    ($SATQ$0dd6227f$SATQ$, $SATQ$07807ef4-fe53-474a-92e4-59c6ea6c579e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0f1ba086-f4ef-489c-863f-7351f6917364$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>At how many points do the graphs of the equations <math alttext="y equals x plus 20"><mrow>
	<mi>y</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mi>x</mi>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mn>20</mn>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="y equals 8 x"><mrow>
	<mi>y</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>8</mn>
		<mi>x</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math> intersect in the <em>xy</em>-plane?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"54f4f246-881d-46fd-97f0-f4cef78f6cc7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0bcc8c8e-2b19-417b-9d2b-6edb6754be83","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d45b308b-9f7d-495d-801a-54e4ed451931","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1aaf8cbc-5a24-4a16-b4cb-27e7e179ab80","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0bcc8c8e-2b19-417b-9d2b-6edb6754be83"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. Each given equation is written in slope-intercept form, <math alttext="y equals m x plus b"><mrow>
	<mi>y</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mi>m</mi>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mi>b</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>, where <math alttext="m"><mi>m</mi>
</math> is the slope and&nbsp;<math alttext="left parenthesis 0 comma b right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is the <em>y</em>-intercept of the graph of the equation in the <em>xy</em>-plane. The graphs of two lines that have different slopes will intersect at exactly one point. The graph of the first equation is a line with slope <math alttext="1"><mn>1</mn>
</math>. The graph of the second equation is a line with slope <math alttext="8"><mn>8</mn>
</math>. Since the graphs are lines with different slopes, they will intersect at exactly one point.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations have <math alttext="0"><mn>0</mn>
</math> intersection points only if they are parallel and therefore have the same slope.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane can have only <math alttext="0"><mn>0</mn>
</math>, <math alttext="1"><mn>1</mn>
</math>, or infinitely many points of intersection.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane can have only <math alttext="0"><mn>0</mn>
</math>, <math alttext="1"><mn>1</mn>
</math>, or infinitely many points of intersection.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0dd6227f","external_id":"07807ef4-fe53-474a-92e4-59c6ea6c579e","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"0f1ba086-f4ef-489c-863f-7351f6917364","uId":"2615b496-fb9e-49ce-a810-2ebf29429a8e","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>At how many points do the graphs of the equations <math alttext=\"y equals x plus 20\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>20</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"y equals 8 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>8</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> intersect in the <em>xy</em>-plane?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"54f4f246-881d-46fd-97f0-f4cef78f6cc7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0bcc8c8e-2b19-417b-9d2b-6edb6754be83","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d45b308b-9f7d-495d-801a-54e4ed451931","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1aaf8cbc-5a24-4a16-b4cb-27e7e179ab80","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["0bcc8c8e-2b19-417b-9d2b-6edb6754be83"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. Each given equation is written in slope-intercept form, <math alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>m</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mi>b</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi>\n</math> is the slope and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma b right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is the <em>y</em>-intercept of the graph of the equation in the <em>xy</em>-plane. The graphs of two lines that have different slopes will intersect at exactly one point. The graph of the first equation is a line with slope <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math>. The graph of the second equation is a line with slope <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>. Since the graphs are lines with different slopes, they will intersect at exactly one point.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations have <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math> intersection points only if they are parallel and therefore have the same slope.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane can have only <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math>, or infinitely many points of intersection.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane can have only <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math>, or infinitely many points of intersection.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959818,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"0dd6227f","skill_cd":"H.D.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","createDate":1691007959818,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":null,"external_id":"07807ef4-fe53-474a-92e4-59c6ea6c579e","primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"2615b496-fb9e-49ce-a810-2ebf29429a8e","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["0bcc8c8e-2b19-417b-9d2b-6edb6754be83"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. Each given equation is written in slope-intercept form, <math alttext=\"y equals m x plus b\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>m</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mi>b</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>, where <math alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi>\n</math> is the slope and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma b right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is the <em>y</em>-intercept of the graph of the equation in the <em>xy</em>-plane. The graphs of two lines that have different slopes will intersect at exactly one point. The graph of the first equation is a line with slope <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math>. The graph of the second equation is a line with slope <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>. Since the graphs are lines with different slopes, they will intersect at exactly one point.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations have <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math> intersection points only if they are parallel and therefore have the same slope.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane can have only <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math>, or infinitely many points of intersection.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect because two graphs of linear equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane can have only <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math>, or infinitely many points of intersection.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p>At how many points do the graphs of the equations <math alttext=\"y equals x plus 20\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mn>20</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"y equals 8 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>8</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> intersect in the <em>xy</em>-plane?</p>","externalid":"07807ef4-fe53-474a-92e4-59c6ea6c579e","templateid":"acf8e7ea-9284-4e84-afa7-a4ac77393642","vaultid":"0f1ba086-f4ef-489c-863f-7351f6917364","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"54f4f246-881d-46fd-97f0-f4cef78f6cc7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0bcc8c8e-2b19-417b-9d2b-6edb6754be83","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d45b308b-9f7d-495d-801a-54e4ed451931","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1aaf8cbc-5a24-4a16-b4cb-27e7e179ab80","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1691007959818,"updateDate":1691007959818}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959818, 1691007959818),
    ($SATQ$0df106df$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$10239-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">An online bookstore sells novels and magazines. Each novel sells for $4, and each magazine sells for $1. If Sadie purchased a total of 11&nbsp;novels and magazines that have a combined selling price of $20, how many novels did she purchase?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p>2</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>3</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>4</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>5</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class="italic">n</span> be the number of novels and <em>m </em>be the number of magazines that Sadie purchased. If Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines, then <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_000_8d4d1542.png" alt="n plus m, equals 11"></span>. It is given that the combined price of 11 novels and magazines is $20. Since each novel sells for $4 and each magazine sells for $1, it follows that <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_001_5ce705bd.png" alt="4 n plus m, equals 20"></span>. So the system of equations below must hold.<p><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_002_29871db3.png" alt="4 n plus m, equals 20; n plus m, equals 11"></span></p><p>Subtracting corresponding sides of the second equation from the first equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_003_369e3e41.png" alt="3 n equals 9"></span>,&nbsp;so <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_004_4dbc588a.png" alt="n equals 3"></span>. Therefore, Sadie purchased 3 novels.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. If 2 novels were purchased, then a total of $8 was spent on novels. That leaves $12 to be spent on magazines, which means that 12 magazines would have been purchased. However, Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines. Choices C and D are incorrect. If 4 novels were purchased, then a total of $16 was spent on novels. That leaves $4 to be spent on magazines, which means that 4 magazines would have been purchased. By the same logic, if Sadie purchased 5 novels, she would have no money at all ($0) to buy magazines. However, Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 5, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"0df106df","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"10239-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">An online bookstore sells novels and magazines. Each novel sells for $4, and each magazine sells for $1. If Sadie purchased a total of 11&nbsp;novels and magazines that have a combined selling price of $20, how many novels did she purchase?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p>2</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>3</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>4</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>5</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">n</span> be the number of novels and <em>m </em>be the number of magazines that Sadie purchased. If Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines, then <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_000_8d4d1542.png\" alt=\"n plus m, equals 11\"></span>. It is given that the combined price of 11 novels and magazines is $20. Since each novel sells for $4 and each magazine sells for $1, it follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_001_5ce705bd.png\" alt=\"4 n plus m, equals 20\"></span>. So the system of equations below must hold.<p><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_002_29871db3.png\" alt=\"4 n plus m, equals 20; n plus m, equals 11\"></span></p><p>Subtracting corresponding sides of the second equation from the first equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_003_369e3e41.png\" alt=\"3 n equals 9\"></span>,&nbsp;so <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/0df106df/img_004_4dbc588a.png\" alt=\"n equals 3\"></span>. Therefore, Sadie purchased 3 novels.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. If 2 novels were purchased, then a total of $8 was spent on novels. That leaves $12 to be spent on magazines, which means that 12 magazines would have been purchased. However, Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines. Choices C and D are incorrect. If 4 novels were purchased, then a total of $16 was spent on novels. That leaves $4 to be spent on magazines, which means that 4 magazines would have been purchased. By the same logic, if Sadie purchased 5 novels, she would have no money at all ($0) to buy magazines. However, Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":5,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959637,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"0df106df","skill_cd":"H.D.","score_band_range_cd":3,"skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","createDate":1691007959637,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"10239-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"e3571810-6e3a-4189-b0c7-fab01a1d1825","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"10239-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">An online bookstore sells novels and magazines. Each novel sells for $4, and each magazine sells for $1. If Sadie purchased a total of 11&nbsp;novels and magazines that have a combined selling price of $20, how many novels did she purchase?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p>2</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p>3</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p>4</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p>5</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"b","rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">n</span> be the number of novels and <em>m </em>be the number of magazines that Sadie purchased. If Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines, then <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"n plus m, equals 11\"></span>. It is given that the combined price of 11 novels and magazines is $20. Since each novel sells for $4 and each magazine sells for $1, it follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 n plus m, equals 20\"></span>. So the system of equations below must hold.<p><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 n plus m, equals 20; n plus m, equals 11\"></span></p><p>Subtracting corresponding sides of the second equation from the first equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"3 n equals 9\"></span>,&nbsp;so <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"n equals 3\"></span>. Therefore, Sadie purchased 3 novels.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. If 2 novels were purchased, then a total of $8 was spent on novels. That leaves $12 to be spent on magazines, which means that 12 magazines would have been purchased. However, Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines. Choices C and D are incorrect. If 4 novels were purchased, then a total of $16 was spent on novels. That leaves $4 to be spent on magazines, which means that 4 magazines would have been purchased. By the same logic, if Sadie purchased 5 novels, she would have no money at all ($0) to buy magazines. However, Sadie purchased a total of 11 novels and magazines.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959637,"updateDate":1691007959637}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959637, 1691007959637),
